  ],
  "hints": [
    "The peak is the largest value, so the answer is the position where that value sits: scan the array and remember the best index seen so far.",
    "Update the remembered index only on a strictly greater value, because on a tie the earlier index is the one the statement asks for.",
    "The brute force can ask the definition directly by checking each index against the whole array, while a single left-to-right pass carrying the best index gets the same answer without rescanning."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force asks the definition directly: for every index it scans the whole array to confirm that no other entry is larger, and returns the first index that survives that check, which is the leftmost occurrence of the maximum. That costs an O(n) rescan per candidate and O(n^2) time overall with no extra storage. The optimal solution carries the running maximum instead of recomputing it: one left-to-right pass holds the best index seen so far and replaces it only when a strictly larger value appears. Requiring strictly larger is what makes the tie rule fall out for free, since an equal value can never displace the earliest index that already reached the maximum, so the single pass returns the same index the brute force finds, in O(n) time and O(1) space.",
    "why_optimal": "The maximum of an array is a global property, so any correct solution has to read every element at least once before it can be sure no later entry is larger: Omega(n) reads are unavoidable. One pass that compares each element against the running best stops exactly at that bound, so the O(n) implementation is optimal up to the single comparison per element, and it needs no working memory beyond two integers because the answer is a position rather than a value. This is also why the element bounds do not buy anything here: the array is arbitrary (unsorted, possibly with repeats), so no halving argument applies and even a sorted-input binary search would first have to pay the O(n log n) sort, which is worse than the linear scan it is meant to speed up.",
    "pitfalls": "Updating the best index with >= instead of > silently shifts ties to the last occurrence, so [4, 9, 2, 9] answers 3 instead of 1 and the two implementations stop agreeing. Starting the search at index 1 while initialising the best index to 0 is right, but initialising it to a sentinel and then comparing nums[i] > nums[sentinel] breaks a single-element array; the empty-array guard must return -1 rather than let an index run out of range. Comparing values with the default string coercion that JavaScript sorts use orders \"100\" before \"9\" and selects the wrong peak, so the running maximum has to compare numbers. The brute force must use the same strict comparison as the optimal, otherwise it keeps a later index on a tie. Finally the answer is an index, not a value: returning nums[peak] answers a different question, and the leftmost tie rule means the array must never be scanned from the right."
  },
